T6 have been discarded by across player? Across player discarded T4 and T1, not T6. T6 is still relatively safe? But T5 is a central tile, might be risky for giving Chow. B5 is central, also risky. W6 has been discarded by across player, so maybe W6 is relatively safe. W9 discarded by across player, so W9 might be safe. B9 discarded by previous player, so B9 might be safe. But B9 is terminal, and T9 is exhausted, so B9 might be desired for sequences? Actually B9 is terminal, could be used for Pung or Chow with B7-B8. But B9 has 3 left, one with me, so 2 left.  \\n\\nI think the best discard is one that is relatively safe and doesn't break promising sequences. My T4,T5,T6 is a complete sequence already, so I could consider discarding one of them if I want to break it, but keeping it gives me a ready meld. Actually T4,T5,T6 is three tiles that form a Chow, so if I keep them together, they are a potential meld. But since I already have an exposed meld, I need three more melds and a pair. So keeping T4,T5,T6 as a potential concealed Chow is good.  \\n\\nI have a pair of W8, which could be my pair. Then I need two more melds. Possibilities: W5,W6 + W7 to form Chow; W8,W9 + W7 to form Chow; B8,B9 + B7 to form Chow. Or I could get Pungs. But Pungs are less likely.  \\n\\nSo I should keep tiles that have good connectivity. W5 is already in a meld, so the extra W5 is less useful unless I go for Kong, but that's unlikely. Discarding W5 might be good because it's a middle tile but already used. However, W5 could also be used with W4,W6? Actually W4 is in the meld, so not available. So W5 is somewhat redundant.  \\n\\nW6 is connected to W5 and W7, but W5 is already in meld, so W6 needs W7 and W8? Actually W6 can form Chow with W4,W5 or W5,W7 or W7,W8. But W4,W5 are in meld, so only W5,W7 or W7,W8. I have W8, so W6,W7,W8 is possible if I draw W7. That's good. So keeping W6 and W8 is beneficial.  \\n\\nW9 is terminal, connected only to W7,W8. I have W8, so if I draw W7, I have W7