rm a pair and a chow or pung. For example, if I get W2, I could form chow W1,W2,W3? But I already have W3 in a meld? Actually, the meld [W3,W4,W5] uses W3, so W3 is not available from my hand. But I could still draw W2 to form W1,W2,W3 chow? But W3 is already used in an exposed meld, but that doesn't prevent me from having another W3 in hand? Actually, there are 4 copies of each tile. So it's possible. But it's uncertain.  \\n\\nAlternatively, if I don't Chi, I just pass, then I draw a tile and discard one. My current hand is not very close to winning; I have only 2 melds and 7 hand tiles that are not well-connected. Chi T5 would give me a third meld, leaving me with 4 tiles that are disconnected (W1, W5, W8, T2). That might be a step towards a winning hand, but it exposes my chow, reducing flexibility. However, since I already have two exposed chows, my hand is already somewhat open.  \\n\\nConsidering scoring: My melds are all W suit chows, so I could aim for Half Flush (honors + one suit) if I get rid of T tiles. But I have T tiles in hand. Chi T5 would actually add a T suit chow, making it mixed suits, reducing chance for Half Flush or Full Flush. But maybe I can go for All Chows? I would have three chows already, need one more chow and a pair. After Chi T5, I'd have three chows (two W chows and one T chow), need one more chow and a pair. With hand tiles W1,W5,W8,T2, I could try to form a chow with W1,W2,W3 or W6,W7,W8 (but W8 is in hand, and I already have a W6,W7,W8 meld, so maybe another W6,W7,W8? Possible but need W6 and W7). Or pair up one of them.  \\n\\nThe alternative is to pass and keep my hand closed, hoping to draw better tiles. But my hand is already not fully concealed because I have exposed chows. So passing might be safer to avoid further exposure, but Chi gives me progress.  \\n\\nAlso, note that the valid action is Chi T5, meaning the previous player discarded a T5? Actually, the valid actions are [Chi T5; Pass]. So the previous player discarded